Planned Unit Development (PUD) Elements

The application is compatible with nearby property, specifically considering existing zoning, the Comprehensive Plan, and any other official plans and policies
created under the guidance of that plan for these areas (2.2.2.d.8).

The suitability of the subject property for development according to the standards and uses of the existing zoning district, compared with the suitability of the
subject property for development according to the standards and uses of proposed zoning district (2.2.2.d.9).

The ability of the application to support the Intent and Applicability statements of the proposed zoning district, and achieve the Design Objectives of any
specific standards (2.2.2.d.10).

The ability of all potential future development to meet all applicable standards for development in the proposed zoning district, including other development
options in the event that any specific development proposed in association with the application is not executed after approval (2.2.2.d.11).

Whether the planning and urban design concepts, and any proposed deviation from or alternative to otherwise applicable development standards, result in
clear public benefits, as opposed to benefits to existing or future land owners in the development (2.2.2.d.12).

The review and recommendations of other reviewing bodies (2.2.2.d.13).
(Not applicable at time of application)
Whether the application will harm the public health, safety, or general welfare (2.2.2.d.14).

The application is compatible with the character of the surrounding area in terms of building scale, building form, landscape, and site design (2.2.2.d.15).

Uses by right and location (5.6.2.f.1).

Uses permitted by Board approval and location (5.6.2.f.2).

Lot and building standards, specifying where they are different from the most similar base zoning district requirements. Lot and building standards shall
include the following for each different lot and building type in the plan: (a) Lot frontage; (b) Lot size; (c) Lot coverage; (d) Building height; and (e) Setback,
build-to lines, or other building orientation specifications (5.6.2.f.3).

Building design standards, specifying where they are different from what would be required by Section 6.6 for residential buildings and lots, 6.7 for small-scale
commercial buildings and lots, or 6.8 for large-scale commercial buildings or lots (5.6.2.f.4).

Landscape, buffering and screening requirements, specifying where they are different from what would be required by Section 6.3 (5.6.2.f.5).

Common open space or facilities and design types, where they are different from what would be required by Section 4.4 (5.6.2.f.6).

Signage requirements and regulations, specifying where they are different from what would be required by Section 6.5 (5.6.2.f.7).

Parking requirements, specifying where they are different from what would be required by Section 6.2 (5.6.2.f.8).

Development performance standards (5.6.2.f.9).

Proposed ownership of common facilities (5.6.2.f.10).

A statement on how proposed deviations from otherwise required standards support the proposed plan, and result in design excellence and outstanding public
amenity that is otherwise not allowable under general zoning standards (5.6.2.f.11).

Any other requirements and restrictions that meet the intent of this district (5.6.2.f.12).
